window.CET6_BANKS.push({"id": "2016-12-3", "source": "2016年12月真题·第3套", "writing": {"prompt": "Directions: For this part, you are allowed 30 minutes to write a short essay on creation. Your essay should include the importance of creation and measures to be taken to encourage creation. You are required to write at least 150 words but no more than 200 words.", "sample": "Creation, or innovation, has become one of the most frequently discussed topics of our age. In my view, it is of\nvital importance to both individuals and society, and deliberate measures ought to be taken to encourage it.\nThe importance of creation can hardly be overstated. First, it serves as the driving force behind social progress:\nalmost every leap in technology, from the smartphone to artificial intelligence, was born of a creative mind.\nMoreover, in an increasingly competitive world, creativity determines whether a company, or even a nation,\ncan stand out rather than be left behind.\nGiven its significance, effective measures are needed to foster it. To begin with, schools ought to reform rigid,\nexam-oriented teaching and reward students who dare to question and imagine. In addition, the government\nshould increase funding for research and protect intellectual property, so that innovators feel safe to explore.\nOnly when creativity is truly valued can a society keep moving forward."}, "translation": {"prompt": "农业是中国的一个重要产业，从业者超过3亿。中国农业产量全球第一，主要生产水稻、小麦和豆类。虽然中国的农业用地仅占世界的百分之十，但为世界百分之二十的人口提供了粮食。中国7700年前开始种植水稻。早在使用机械和化肥之前，勤劳和富有创造性的中国农民就已经采用各种各样的方法来增加农作物产量。中国农业最新的发展是推进有机农业。有机农业可以同时服务于多种目的，包括食品安全、大众健康和可持续发展。", "sample": "Agriculture is an important industry in China, with over 300 million people engaged in it. China ranks first in the world in agricultural output, producing mainly rice, wheat and legumes. Although China’s farmland accounts for only 10% of the world’s total, it feeds 20% of the world’s population. China began growing rice 7,700 years ago. Long before they used machinery and chemical fertilizers, hardworking and creative Chinese farmers had already adopted various methods to increase crop yields. The latest development in Chinese agriculture is the promotion of organic farming. It can serve multiple purposes simultaneously, including food safety, public health and sustainable development."}, "listeningUrl": null, "clozePassage": "Small communities, with their distinctive character—where life is stable and intensely human—are disappearing. Some have  __26__  from the face of the earth, others are dying slowly, but all have  __27__  changes as they have come into contact with an  __28__  machine civilization. The merging of diverse peoples into a common mass has produced tension among members of the minorities and the majority alike.\nThe Old Order Amish, who arrived on American shores in colonial times, have  __29__  in the modern world in distinctive, small communities. They have resisted the homogenization  __30__  more successfully than others. In planting and harvesting time one can see their bearded men working the fields with horses and their women hanging out the laundry in neat rows to dry. Many American people have seen Amish families, with the men wearing broad-brimmed black hats and the women in long dresses, in railway or bus  __31__  . Although the Amish have lived with  __32__  America for over two and a half centuries, they have moderated its influence on their personal lives, their families, communities, and their values.\nThe Amish are often  __33__  by other Americans to be relics of the past who live a simple, inflexible life dedicated to inconvenient out-dated customs. They are seen as abandoning both modern  __34__  and the American dream of success and progress. But most people have no quarrel with the Amish for doing things the old-fashioned way. Their conscientious objection was tolerated in wartime, for after all, they are good farmers who  __35__  the virtues of work and thrift.", "matchIntro": "Countries Rush for Upper Hand in Antarctica", "matchParagraphs": [{"label": "A", "text": "On a glacier-filled island with fjords(峡湾) and elephant seals, Russia has built Antarctica's first Orthodox church on a hill overlooking its research base. Less than an hour away by snowmobile, Chinese labourers have updated the Great Wall Station, a vital part of China's plan to operate five bases on Antarctica, complete with an indoor badminton court and sleeping quarters for 150 people. Not to be outdone, India's futuristic new Bharathi base, built on stilts(桩子) using 134 interlocking shipping containers, resembles a spaceship. Turkey and Iran have announced plans to build bases, too."}, {"label": "B", "text": "More than a century has passed since explorers raced to plant their flags at the bottom of the world, and for decades to come this continent is supposed to be protected as a scientific preserve, shielded from intrusions like military activities and mining. But an array of countries are rushing to assert greater influence here, with an eye not just towards the day those protective treaties expire, but also for the strategic and commercial opportunities that already exist."}, {"label": "C", "text": "The newer players are stepping into what they view as a treasure house of resources. Some of the ventures focus on the Antarctic resources that are already up for grabs, like abundant sea life. South Korea, which operates state-of-the-art bases here, is increasing its fishing of krill(磷虾), found in abundance in the Southern Ocean, while Russia recently frustrated efforts to create one of the world's largest ocean sanctuaries here."}, {"label": "D", "text": "Some scientists are examining the potential for harvesting icebergs from Antarctica, which is estimated to have the biggest reserves of fresh water on the planet. Nations are also pressing ahead with space research and satellite projects to expand their global navigation abilities."}, {"label": "E", "text": "Building on a Soviet-era foothold, Russia is expanding its monitoring stations for Glonass, its version of the Global Positioning System (GPS). At least three Russian stations are already operating in Antarctica, part of its effort to challenge the dominance of the American GPS, and new stations are planned for sites like the Russian base, in the shadow of the Orthodox Church of the Holy Trinity."}, {"label": "F", "text": "Elsewhere in Antarctica, Russian researchers boast of their recent discovery of a freshwater reserve the size of Lake Ontario after drilling through miles of solid ice. \"You can see that we're here to stay,\" said Vladimir Cheberdak, 57, chief of the Bellingshausen Station, as he sipped tea under a portrait of Fabian Gottlieb von Bellingshausen, a high-ranking officer in the Imperial Russian Navy who explored the Antarctic coast in 1820."}, {"label": "G", "text": "Antarctica's mineral, oil and gas wealth is a longer-term prize. The treaty banning mining here, shielding coveted(令人垂涎的) reserves of iron ore, coal and chromium, comes up for review in 2048. Researchers recently found kimberlite(金伯利岩) deposits hinting at the existence of diamonds. And while assessments vary widely, geologists estimate that Antarctica holds at least 36 billion barrels of oil and natural gas."}, {"label": "H", "text": "Beyond the Antarctic treaties, huge obstacles persist to tapping these resources, like drifting icebergs that could jeopardise offshore platforms. Then there is Antarctica's remoteness, with some mineral deposits found in windswept locations on a continent that is larger than Europe and where winter temperatures hover around minus 55 degrees Celsius."}, {"label": "I", "text": "But advances in technology might make Antarctica a lot more accessible three decades from now. And even before then, scholars warn, the demand for resources in an energy-hungry world could raise pressure to renegotiate Antarctica's treaties, possibly allowing more commercial endeavours here well before the prohibitions against them expire. The research stations on King George Island offer a glimpse into the long game on this ice-blanketed continent as nations assert themselves, eroding the sway long held by countries like the United States, Britain, Australia and New Zealand."}, {"label": "J", "text": "Being stationed in Antarctica involves adapting to life on the planet's driest, windiest and coldest continent, yet each nation manages to make itself at home. Bearded Russian priests offer regular services at the Orthodox church for the 16 or so Russian speakers who spend the winter at the base, largely polar scientists in fields like glaciology and meteorology. Their number climbs to about 40 in the warmer summer months. China has arguably the fastest-growing operations in Antarctica. It opened its fourth station last year and is pressing ahead with plans to build a fifth. It is building its second ice-breaking ship and setting up research drilling operations on an ice dome 13,422 feet above sea level that is one of the planet's coldest places. Chinese officials say the expansion in Antarctica prioritises scientific research, but they also acknowledge that concerns about \"resource security\" influence their moves."}, {"label": "K", "text": "China's newly renovated Great Wall Station on King George Island makes the Russian and Chilean bases here seem outdated. \"We do weather monitoring here and other research,\" Ning Xu, 53, the chief of the Chinese base, said over tea during a fierce blizzard(暴风雪) in late November. The large base he leads resembles a snowed-in college campus on holiday break, with the capacity to sleep more than 10 times the 13 people who were staying on through the Antarctic winter. Yong Yu, a Chinese microbiologist, showed off the spacious building, with empty desks under an illustrated timeline detailing the rapid growth of China's Antarctic operations since the 1980s. \"We now feel equipped to grow,\" he said."}, {"label": "L", "text": "As some countries expand operations in Antarctica, the United States maintains three year-round stations on the continent with more than 1,000 people during the southern hemisphere's summer, including those at the Amundsen-Scott station, built in 1956 at an elevation of 9,301 feet on a plateau at the South Pole. But US researchers quietly complain about budget restraints and having far fewer icebreakers than Russia, limiting the reach of the United States in Antarctica."}, {"label": "M", "text": "Scholars warn that Antarctica's political drift could blur the distinction between military and civilian activities long before the continent's treaties come up for renegotiation, especially in parts of Antarctica that are ideal for intercepting(拦截) signals from satellites or retasking satellite systems, potentially enhancing global electronic intelligence operations."}, {"label": "N", "text": "Some countries have had a hard time here. Brazil opened a research station in 1984, but it was largely destroyed by a fire that killed two members of the navy in 2012, the same year that a diesel-laden Brazilian barge sank near the base. As if that were not enough, a Brazilian C-130 Hercules military transport plane has remained stranded near the runway of Chile's air base here since it crash-landed in 2014."}, {"label": "O", "text": "However, Brazil's stretch of misfortune has created opportunities for China, with a Chinese company winning the $100 million contract in 2015 to rebuild the Brazilian station."}, {"label": "P", "text": "Amid all the changes, Antarctica maintains its allure. South Korea opened its second Antarctic research base in 2014, describing it as a way to test robots developed by Korean researchers for use in extreme conditions. With Russia's help, Belarus is preparing to build its first Antarctic base. Colombia said this year that it planned to join other South American nations with bases in Antarctica."}, {"label": "Q", "text": "\"The old days of the Antarctic being dominated by the interests and wishes of white men from European, Australasian and North American states are over,\" said Klaus Dodds, a politics scholar at the University of London who specialises in Antarctica. \"The reality is that Antarctica is geopolitically contested.\""}], "readingPassages": {"46": "Any veteran nicotine addict will testify that fancy packaging plays no role in the decision to keep smoking. So, it is argued, stripping cartons of their branding will trigger no mass movement to quit.\nBut that isn't why the government—under pressure from cancer charities, health workers and the Labour party—has agreed to legislate for standardised packaging. The theory is that smoking should be stripped of any appeal to discourage new generations from starting in the first place. Plain packaging would be another step in the reclassification of cigarettes from inviting consumer products to narcotics(麻醉剂).\nNaturally, the tobacco industry is violently opposed. No business likes to admit that it sells addictive poison as a lifestyle choice. That is why government has historically intervened, banning advertising, imposing health warnings and punitive(惩罚性的) duties. This approach has led over time to a fall in smoking with numbers having roughly halved since the 1970s. Evidence from Australia suggests plain packaging pushes society further along that road. Since tobacco is one of the biggest causes of premature death in the UK, a measure that tames the habit even by a fraction is worth trying.\nSo why has it taken so long? The Department of Health declared its intention to consider the move in November 2010 and consulted through 2012. But the plan was suspended in July 2013. It did not escape notice that a lobbying firm set up by Lynton Crosby, David Cameron's election campaign director, had previously acted for Philip Morris International. (The prime minister denied there was a connection between his new adviser's outside interests and the change in legislative programme.) In November 2013, after an unnecessary round of additional consultation, health minister Jane Ellison said the government was minded to proceed after all. Now we are told Members of Parliament (MPs) will have a free vote before parliament is dissolved in March.\nParliament has in fact already authorised the government to tame the tobacco trade. MPs voted overwhelmingly in favour of Labour amendments to the children and families bill last February that included the power to regulate for plain packaging. With sufficient will in Downing Street this would have been done already. But strength of will is the missing ingredient where Mr. Cameron and public health are concerned. His attitude to state intervention has looked confused ever since his bizarre 2006 lament(叹惜) that chocolate oranges placed seductively at supermarket checkouts fueled obesity.\nThe government has moved reluctantly into a sensible public health policy, but with such obvious over-cautiousness that any political credit due belongs to the opposition. Without sustained external pressure it seems certain Mr. Cameron would still be hooked on the interests of big tobacco companies.", "47": "Any veteran nicotine addict will testify that fancy packaging plays no role in the decision to keep smoking. So, it is argued, stripping cartons of their branding will trigger no mass movement to quit.\nBut that isn't why the government—under pressure from cancer charities, health workers and the Labour party—has agreed to legislate for standardised packaging. The theory is that smoking should be stripped of any appeal to discourage new generations from starting in the first place. Plain packaging would be another step in the reclassification of cigarettes from inviting consumer products to narcotics(麻醉剂).\nNaturally, the tobacco industry is violently opposed. No business likes to admit that it sells addictive poison as a lifestyle choice. That is why government has historically intervened, banning advertising, imposing health warnings and punitive(惩罚性的) duties. This approach has led over time to a fall in smoking with numbers having roughly halved since the 1970s. Evidence from Australia suggests plain packaging pushes society further along that road. Since tobacco is one of the biggest causes of premature death in the UK, a measure that tames the habit even by a fraction is worth trying.\nSo why has it taken so long? The Department of Health declared its intention to consider the move in November 2010 and consulted through 2012. But the plan was suspended in July 2013. It did not escape notice that a lobbying firm set up by Lynton Crosby, David Cameron's election campaign director, had previously acted for Philip Morris International. (The prime minister denied there was a connection between his new adviser's outside interests and the change in legislative programme.) In November 2013, after an unnecessary round of additional consultation, health minister Jane Ellison said the government was minded to proceed after all. Now we are told Members of Parliament (MPs) will have a free vote before parliament is dissolved in March.\nParliament has in fact already authorised the government to tame the tobacco trade. MPs voted overwhelmingly in favour of Labour amendments to the children and families bill last February that included the power to regulate for plain packaging. With sufficient will in Downing Street this would have been done already. But strength of will is the missing ingredient where Mr. Cameron and public health are concerned. His attitude to state intervention has looked confused ever since his bizarre 2006 lament(叹惜) that chocolate oranges placed seductively at supermarket checkouts fueled obesity.\nThe government has moved reluctantly into a sensible public health policy, but with such obvious over-cautiousness that any political credit due belongs to the opposition. Without sustained external pressure it seems certain Mr. Cameron would still be hooked on the interests of big tobacco companies.", "48": "Any veteran nicotine addict will testify that fancy packaging plays no role in the decision to keep smoking. So, it is argued, stripping cartons of their branding will trigger no mass movement to quit.\nBut that isn't why the government—under pressure from cancer charities, health workers and the Labour party—has agreed to legislate for standardised packaging. The theory is that smoking should be stripped of any appeal to discourage new generations from starting in the first place. Plain packaging would be another step in the reclassification of cigarettes from inviting consumer products to narcotics(麻醉剂).\nNaturally, the tobacco industry is violently opposed. No business likes to admit that it sells addictive poison as a lifestyle choice. That is why government has historically intervened, banning advertising, imposing health warnings and punitive(惩罚性的) duties. This approach has led over time to a fall in smoking with numbers having roughly halved since the 1970s. Evidence from Australia suggests plain packaging pushes society further along that road. Since tobacco is one of the biggest causes of premature death in the UK, a measure that tames the habit even by a fraction is worth trying.\nSo why has it taken so long? The Department of Health declared its intention to consider the move in November 2010 and consulted through 2012. But the plan was suspended in July 2013. It did not escape notice that a lobbying firm set up by Lynton Crosby, David Cameron's election campaign director, had previously acted for Philip Morris International. (The prime minister denied there was a connection between his new adviser's outside interests and the change in legislative programme.) In November 2013, after an unnecessary round of additional consultation, health minister Jane Ellison said the government was minded to proceed after all. Now we are told Members of Parliament (MPs) will have a free vote before parliament is dissolved in March.\nParliament has in fact already authorised the government to tame the tobacco trade. MPs voted overwhelmingly in favour of Labour amendments to the children and families bill last February that included the power to regulate for plain packaging. With sufficient will in Downing Street this would have been done already. But strength of will is the missing ingredient where Mr. Cameron and public health are concerned. His attitude to state intervention has looked confused ever since his bizarre 2006 lament(叹惜) that chocolate oranges placed seductively at supermarket checkouts fueled obesity.\nThe government has moved reluctantly into a sensible public health policy, but with such obvious over-cautiousness that any political credit due belongs to the opposition. Without sustained external pressure it seems certain Mr. Cameron would still be hooked on the interests of big tobacco companies.", "49": "Any veteran nicotine addict will testify that fancy packaging plays no role in the decision to keep smoking. So, it is argued, stripping cartons of their branding will trigger no mass movement to quit.\nBut that isn't why the government—under pressure from cancer charities, health workers and the Labour party—has agreed to legislate for standardised packaging. The theory is that smoking should be stripped of any appeal to discourage new generations from starting in the first place. Plain packaging would be another step in the reclassification of cigarettes from inviting consumer products to narcotics(麻醉剂).\nNaturally, the tobacco industry is violently opposed. No business likes to admit that it sells addictive poison as a lifestyle choice. That is why government has historically intervened, banning advertising, imposing health warnings and punitive(惩罚性的) duties. This approach has led over time to a fall in smoking with numbers having roughly halved since the 1970s. Evidence from Australia suggests plain packaging pushes society further along that road. Since tobacco is one of the biggest causes of premature death in the UK, a measure that tames the habit even by a fraction is worth trying.\nSo why has it taken so long? The Department of Health declared its intention to consider the move in November 2010 and consulted through 2012. But the plan was suspended in July 2013. It did not escape notice that a lobbying firm set up by Lynton Crosby, David Cameron's election campaign director, had previously acted for Philip Morris International. (The prime minister denied there was a connection between his new adviser's outside interests and the change in legislative programme.) In November 2013, after an unnecessary round of additional consultation, health minister Jane Ellison said the government was minded to proceed after all. Now we are told Members of Parliament (MPs) will have a free vote before parliament is dissolved in March.\nParliament has in fact already authorised the government to tame the tobacco trade. MPs voted overwhelmingly in favour of Labour amendments to the children and families bill last February that included the power to regulate for plain packaging. With sufficient will in Downing Street this would have been done already. But strength of will is the missing ingredient where Mr. Cameron and public health are concerned. His attitude to state intervention has looked confused ever since his bizarre 2006 lament(叹惜) that chocolate oranges placed seductively at supermarket checkouts fueled obesity.\nThe government has moved reluctantly into a sensible public health policy, but with such obvious over-cautiousness that any political credit due belongs to the opposition. Without sustained external pressure it seems certain Mr. Cameron would still be hooked on the interests of big tobacco companies.", "50": "Any veteran nicotine addict will testify that fancy packaging plays no role in the decision to keep smoking. So, it is argued, stripping cartons of their branding will trigger no mass movement to quit.\nBut that isn't why the government—under pressure from cancer charities, health workers and the Labour party—has agreed to legislate for standardised packaging. The theory is that smoking should be stripped of any appeal to discourage new generations from starting in the first place. Plain packaging would be another step in the reclassification of cigarettes from inviting consumer products to narcotics(麻醉剂).\nNaturally, the tobacco industry is violently opposed. No business likes to admit that it sells addictive poison as a lifestyle choice. That is why government has historically intervened, banning advertising, imposing health warnings and punitive(惩罚性的) duties. This approach has led over time to a fall in smoking with numbers having roughly halved since the 1970s. Evidence from Australia suggests plain packaging pushes society further along that road. Since tobacco is one of the biggest causes of premature death in the UK, a measure that tames the habit even by a fraction is worth trying.\nSo why has it taken so long? The Department of Health declared its intention to consider the move in November 2010 and consulted through 2012. But the plan was suspended in July 2013. It did not escape notice that a lobbying firm set up by Lynton Crosby, David Cameron's election campaign director, had previously acted for Philip Morris International. (The prime minister denied there was a connection between his new adviser's outside interests and the change in legislative programme.) In November 2013, after an unnecessary round of additional consultation, health minister Jane Ellison said the government was minded to proceed after all. Now we are told Members of Parliament (MPs) will have a free vote before parliament is dissolved in March.\nParliament has in fact already authorised the government to tame the tobacco trade. MPs voted overwhelmingly in favour of Labour amendments to the children and families bill last February that included the power to regulate for plain packaging. With sufficient will in Downing Street this would have been done already. But strength of will is the missing ingredient where Mr. Cameron and public health are concerned. His attitude to state intervention has looked confused ever since his bizarre 2006 lament(叹惜) that chocolate oranges placed seductively at supermarket checkouts fueled obesity.\nThe government has moved reluctantly into a sensible public health policy, but with such obvious over-cautiousness that any political credit due belongs to the opposition. Without sustained external pressure it seems certain Mr. Cameron would still be hooked on the interests of big tobacco companies.", "51": "What a waste of money! In return for an average of £44,000 of debt, students get an average of only 14 hours of lecture and tutorial time a week in Britain. Annual fees have risen from £1,000 to £9,000 in the last decade, but contact time at university has barely risen at all. And graduating doesn't even provide any guarantee of a decent job: six in ten graduates today are in non-graduate jobs.\nNo wonder it has become fashionable to denounce many universities as little more than elaborate con-tricks(骗术). There's a lot for students to complain about: the repayment threshold for paying back loans will be frozen for five years, meaning that lower-paid graduates have to start repaying their loans; and maintenance grants have been replaced by loans, meaning that students from poorer backgrounds face higher debt than those with wealthier parents.\nYet it still pays to go to university. If going to university doesn't work out, students pay very little—if any—of their tuition fees back: you only start repaying when you are earning £21,000 a year. Almost half of graduates—those who go on to earn less—will have a portion of their debt written off. It's not just the lectures and tutorials that are important. Education is the sum of what students teach each other in between lectures and seminars. Students do not merely benefit while at university; studies show—they go on to be healthier and happier than non-graduates, and also far more likely to vote.\nWhatever your talents, it is extraordinarily difficult to get a leading job in most fields without having been to university. Recruiters circle elite universities like vultures(兀鹰). Many top firms will not even look at applications from those who lack a 2.1, i.e., an upper-second class degree, from an elite university. Students at university also meet those likely to be in leading jobs in the future, forming contacts for life. This might not be right, but school-leavers who fail to acknowledge as much risk making the wrong decision about going to university.\nPerhaps the reason why so many universities offer their students so little is they know studying at a top university remains a brilliant investment even if you don't learn anything. Studying at university will only become less attractive if employers shift their focus away from where someone went to university—and there is no sign of that happening anytime soon. School-leavers may moan, but they have little choice but to embrace university and the student debt that comes with it.", "52": "What a waste of money! In return for an average of £44,000 of debt, students get an average of only 14 hours of lecture and tutorial time a week in Britain. Annual fees have risen from £1,000 to £9,000 in the last decade, but contact time at university has barely risen at all. And graduating doesn't even provide any guarantee of a decent job: six in ten graduates today are in non-graduate jobs.\nNo wonder it has become fashionable to denounce many universities as little more than elaborate con-tricks(骗术). There's a lot for students to complain about: the repayment threshold for paying back loans will be frozen for five years, meaning that lower-paid graduates have to start repaying their loans; and maintenance grants have been replaced by loans, meaning that students from poorer backgrounds face higher debt than those with wealthier parents.\nYet it still pays to go to university. If going to university doesn't work out, students pay very little—if any—of their tuition fees back: you only start repaying when you are earning £21,000 a year. Almost half of graduates—those who go on to earn less—will have a portion of their debt written off. It's not just the lectures and tutorials that are important. Education is the sum of what students teach each other in between lectures and seminars. Students do not merely benefit while at university; studies show—they go on to be healthier and happier than non-graduates, and also far more likely to vote.\nWhatever your talents, it is extraordinarily difficult to get a leading job in most fields without having been to university. Recruiters circle elite universities like vultures(兀鹰). Many top firms will not even look at applications from those who lack a 2.1, i.e., an upper-second class degree, from an elite university. Students at university also meet those likely to be in leading jobs in the future, forming contacts for life. This might not be right, but school-leavers who fail to acknowledge as much risk making the wrong decision about going to university.\nPerhaps the reason why so many universities offer their students so little is they know studying at a top university remains a brilliant investment even if you don't learn anything. Studying at university will only become less attractive if employers shift their focus away from where someone went to university—and there is no sign of that happening anytime soon. School-leavers may moan, but they have little choice but to embrace university and the student debt that comes with it.", "53": "What a waste of money! In return for an average of £44,000 of debt, students get an average of only 14 hours of lecture and tutorial time a week in Britain. Annual fees have risen from £1,000 to £9,000 in the last decade, but contact time at university has barely risen at all. And graduating doesn't even provide any guarantee of a decent job: six in ten graduates today are in non-graduate jobs.\nNo wonder it has become fashionable to denounce many universities as little more than elaborate con-tricks(骗术). There's a lot for students to complain about: the repayment threshold for paying back loans will be frozen for five years, meaning that lower-paid graduates have to start repaying their loans; and maintenance grants have been replaced by loans, meaning that students from poorer backgrounds face higher debt than those with wealthier parents.\nYet it still pays to go to university. If going to university doesn't work out, students pay very little—if any—of their tuition fees back: you only start repaying when you are earning £21,000 a year. Almost half of graduates—those who go on to earn less—will have a portion of their debt written off. It's not just the lectures and tutorials that are important. Education is the sum of what students teach each other in between lectures and seminars. Students do not merely benefit while at university; studies show—they go on to be healthier and happier than non-graduates, and also far more likely to vote.\nWhatever your talents, it is extraordinarily difficult to get a leading job in most fields without having been to university. Recruiters circle elite universities like vultures(兀鹰). Many top firms will not even look at applications from those who lack a 2.1, i.e., an upper-second class degree, from an elite university. Students at university also meet those likely to be in leading jobs in the future, forming contacts for life. This might not be right, but school-leavers who fail to acknowledge as much risk making the wrong decision about going to university.\nPerhaps the reason why so many universities offer their students so little is they know studying at a top university remains a brilliant investment even if you don't learn anything. Studying at university will only become less attractive if employers shift their focus away from where someone went to university—and there is no sign of that happening anytime soon. School-leavers may moan, but they have little choice but to embrace university and the student debt that comes with it.", "54": "What a waste of money! In return for an average of £44,000 of debt, students get an average of only 14 hours of lecture and tutorial time a week in Britain. Annual fees have risen from £1,000 to £9,000 in the last decade, but contact time at university has barely risen at all. And graduating doesn't even provide any guarantee of a decent job: six in ten graduates today are in non-graduate jobs.\nNo wonder it has become fashionable to denounce many universities as little more than elaborate con-tricks(骗术). There's a lot for students to complain about: the repayment threshold for paying back loans will be frozen for five years, meaning that lower-paid graduates have to start repaying their loans; and maintenance grants have been replaced by loans, meaning that students from poorer backgrounds face higher debt than those with wealthier parents.\nYet it still pays to go to university. If going to university doesn't work out, students pay very little—if any—of their tuition fees back: you only start repaying when you are earning £21,000 a year. Almost half of graduates—those who go on to earn less—will have a portion of their debt written off. It's not just the lectures and tutorials that are important. Education is the sum of what students teach each other in between lectures and seminars. Students do not merely benefit while at university; studies show—they go on to be healthier and happier than non-graduates, and also far more likely to vote.\nWhatever your talents, it is extraordinarily difficult to get a leading job in most fields without having been to university. Recruiters circle elite universities like vultures(兀鹰). Many top firms will not even look at applications from those who lack a 2.1, i.e., an upper-second class degree, from an elite university. Students at university also meet those likely to be in leading jobs in the future, forming contacts for life. This might not be right, but school-leavers who fail to acknowledge as much risk making the wrong decision about going to university.\nPerhaps the reason why so many universities offer their students so little is they know studying at a top university remains a brilliant investment even if you don't learn anything. Studying at university will only become less attractive if employers shift their focus away from where someone went to university—and there is no sign of that happening anytime soon. School-leavers may moan, but they have little choice but to embrace university and the student debt that comes with it.", "55": "What a waste of money! In return for an average of £44,000 of debt, students get an average of only 14 hours of lecture and tutorial time a week in Britain. Annual fees have risen from £1,000 to £9,000 in the last decade, but contact time at university has barely risen at all. And graduating doesn't even provide any guarantee of a decent job: six in ten graduates today are in non-graduate jobs.\nNo wonder it has become fashionable to denounce many universities as little more than elaborate con-tricks(骗术). There's a lot for students to complain about: the repayment threshold for paying back loans will be frozen for five years, meaning that lower-paid graduates have to start repaying their loans; and maintenance grants have been replaced by loans, meaning that students from poorer backgrounds face higher debt than those with wealthier parents.\nYet it still pays to go to university. If going to university doesn't work out, students pay very little—if any—of their tuition fees back: you only start repaying when you are earning £21,000 a year. Almost half of graduates—those who go on to earn less—will have a portion of their debt written off. It's not just the lectures and tutorials that are important. Education is the sum of what students teach each other in between lectures and seminars. Students do not merely benefit while at university; studies show—they go on to be healthier and happier than non-graduates, and also far more likely to vote.\nWhatever your talents, it is extraordinarily difficult to get a leading job in most fields without having been to university. Recruiters circle elite universities like vultures(兀鹰). Many top firms will not even look at applications from those who lack a 2.1, i.e., an upper-second class degree, from an elite university. Students at university also meet those likely to be in leading jobs in the future, forming contacts for life. This might not be right, but school-leavers who fail to acknowledge as much risk making the wrong decision about going to university.\nPerhaps the reason why so many universities offer their students so little is they know studying at a top university remains a brilliant investment even if you don't learn anything. Studying at university will only become less attractive if employers shift their focus away from where someone went to university—and there is no sign of that happening anytime soon. School-leavers may moan, but they have little choice but to embrace university and the student debt that comes with it."}, "questions": [{"id": "2016-12-3-c-26", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 26, "stem": "", "options": ["A) accessing", "B) conveniences", "C) destined", "D) expanding", "E) industrialized", "F) perceived", "G) practice", "H) process", "I) progress", "J) respective", "K) survived", "L) terminals", "M) undergone", "N) universal", "O) vanished"], "answer": "O", "analysis": "【答案】O) vanished\n动词\n【词性槽】have + 空 + from，空作现在完成时的过去分词\n【依据】vanish from = 从…消失，与上下文\"消失\"主线呼应\nSmall communities, with their distinctive character—where life is stable and\nintensely human—are disappearing.点题\"消失\"，紧接Some have __(26)__ from the\nface of the earth, others are dying slowly，与 disappearing 同义、与 dying slowly 形\n成程度递进，vanish from 是固定搭配。\n【竞争词】F) perceived 及物动词且义不通\nperceive 通常及物，意为\"察觉到\"，与 from 不搭，也与小社区\"消失\"的语义无关。\nK) survived 感情色彩相反\nsurvive 表\"幸存\"，与并列分句 others are dying slowly 形成\"幸存 vs 慢慢死去\"的反义对照，\n但前面 disappearing 已定调\"消失\"，本句需要的是与 dying 同向的递进词。\nM) undergone 及物且义不切题\nundergo（经历）通常及物，需直接接宾语如 undergo changes，与 from 搭配不自然。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 K) —— survived 与 vanished 感情色彩相反。26 题 Some have\n__(26)__ from the face of the earth, others are dying slowly中，survived与 from 也能\n搭（survive from），且\"幸存\"与\"渐死\"看似对照诱人，但前句 disappearing 已定调\"消\n失\"，本句需与 dying slowly 形成程度递进而非反义。防坑：先看上一句基调，再决定动词\n同向还是反向。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2016-12-3-c-27", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 27, "stem": "", "options": ["A) accessing", "B) conveniences", "C) destined", "D) expanding", "E) industrialized", "F) perceived", "G) practice", "H) process", "I) progress", "J) respective", "K) survived", "L) terminals", "M) undergone", "N) universal", "O) vanished"], "answer": "M", "analysis": "【答案】M) undergone\n动词\n【词性槽】have + 空 + changes，空作现在完成时及物动词的过去分词\n【依据】undergo changes = 经历变化，固定搭配\nbut all have __(27)__ changes as they have come into contact with an __(28)__\nmachine civilization.描述小社区在接触机器文明的过程中\"经历变化\"，undergo changes\n是常见标准搭配，与 as 引导的原因状语吻合。\n【竞争词】F) perceived 语义不通\nperceived changes 意为\"感知到变化\"，但句子主语是 communities（小社区）本身，主语不\n会\"感知\"变化，而是\"经历\"变化。\nK) survived 搭配不成立\nsurvive changes（经受住变化）虽语法可通，但与 but 引导的让步逻辑（有的消失、有的渐\n死，但都……）不一致——这里需要的是\"都受到了影响\"，而非\"都活下来了\"。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2016-12-3-c-28", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 28, "stem": "", "options": ["A) accessing", "B) conveniences", "C) destined", "D) expanding", "E) industrialized", "F) perceived", "G) practice", "H) process", "I) progress", "J) respective", "K) survived", "L) terminals", "M) undergone", "N) universal", "O) vanished"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D) expanding\n形容词\n【词性槽】an + 空 + machine civilization，空作 machine civilization 的前置定语\n【依据】an expanding machine civilization = 不断扩张的机器文明\nSmall communities, with their distinctive character—where life is stable and\nintensely human—are disappearing.点明小社区在消失，原因正是they have come into\ncontact with an __(28)__ machine civilization.——一个不断扩张的机器文明吞噬小社\n区，expanding 既满足 an 后元音音素，也契合\"扩张\"压迫小社区的因果。\n【竞争词】C) destined 搭配不自然且首音非元音\ndestined machine civilization 不构成自然表达，且 destined 以辅音开头与 an 不匹配。\nJ) respective 义不切题\nrespective 表\"各自的\"，需复数语境，且 an respective 也读音不顺。\nN) universal 首音非元音、义偏\nuniversal 读音以辅音 /j/ 起，与 an 不搭；且\"普遍的机器文明\"不如\"扩张的机器文明\"贴合\"小社\n区被冲击\"的因果。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2016-12-3-c-29", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 29, "stem": "", "options": ["A) accessing", "B) conveniences", "C) destined", "D) expanding", "E) industrialized", "F) perceived", "G) practice", "H) process", "I) progress", "J) respective", "K) survived", "L) terminals", "M) undergone", "N) universal", "O) vanished"], "answer": "K", "analysis": "【答案】K) survived\n动词\n【词性槽】have + 空 + in，空作现在完成时不及物动词的过去分词\n【依据】survive in = 在…中存续，呼应主题反例\nThe Old Order Amish, who arrived on American shores in colonial times, have\n__(29)__ in the modern world in distinctive, small communities.描述阿米什人作为\"小社\n区在现代化中消失\"的反例，他们\"在现代世界里以小社区方式存续\"，survive in 与 P1 主题\n形成对照。\n【竞争词】F) perceived 义不通且需补语\nperceive 表\"感知/认为\"，若作被动需补 as + 名词或形容词，本句没有该补语，且\"感知在现代\n世界里\"语义不通。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2016-12-3-c-30", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 30, "stem": "", "options": ["A) accessing", "B) conveniences", "C) destined", "D) expanding", "E) industrialized", "F) perceived", "G) practice", "H) process", "I) progress", "J) respective", "K) survived", "L) terminals", "M) undergone", "N) universal", "O) vanished"], "answer": "H", "analysis": "【答案】H) process\n名词\n【词性槽】the + 空 + 比较状语，空作 homogenization 的同位/补语名词\n【依据】homogenization process = 同质化进程，固定搭配\n原文谈They have resisted the homogenization __(30)__ more successfully than\nothers.，与首段all have __(27)__ changes as they have come into contact with an\n__(28)__ machine civilization呼应，作者强调阿米什人在现代化\"进程\"中抵抗同质化，\nprocess 与 homogenization 构成常见搭配 homogenization process。\n【竞争词】B) conveniences 义不通且为复数\nconveniences 指便利设施，与 homogenization（同质化）不构成语义搭配，也无法解释\"抵\n抗\"的对象。\nI) progress 感情色彩与逻辑相反\nprogress 是褒义\"进步\"，阿米什人抵抗的不是进步本身，而是把所有人变成一样的\"进程\"，且\nhomogenization progress 不构成自然搭配。\nL) terminals 义项不匹配且为复数\nterminals 指终点站/航站楼，是具体名词，无法与抽象名词 homogenization 复合。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 I) —— process vs progress 形近混淆。30 题 the\nhomogenization ___ 处，progress与process形近且都为名词，主体考生极易选成\nprogress。防坑：homogenization process（同质化进程）是固定搭配，且本句主语\n是\"抵抗\"，宾语应为中性\"进程\"而非褒义\"进步\"。", "points": ["选词填空", "名词"]}, {"id": "2016-12-3-c-31", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 31, "stem": "", "options": ["A) accessing", "B) conveniences", "C) destined", "D) expanding", "E) industrialized", "F) perceived", "G) practice", "H) process", "I) progress", "J) respective", "K) survived", "L) terminals", "M) undergone", "N) universal", "O) vanished"], "answer": "L", "analysis": "【答案】L) terminals\n名词\n【词性槽】介词 in + railway or bus ___，空作介词宾语中心名词\n【依据】railway/bus terminals = 火车站/汽车终点站，标准搭配\n原文描述美国人常在in railway or bus __(31)__见到阿米什家庭，terminals 既能与 railway\n（火车终点站）也能与 bus（汽车终点站）搭配，且为复数与 or 连接的两类终点呼应。\n【竞争词】B) conveniences 搭配与语义不成立\nconveniences 不能与 railway/bus 构成\"火车便利/公交便利\"的合理表达，也无法承担\"地\n点\"语义。\nI) progress 抽象名词与地点语义冲突\nprogress 是抽象进步义，无法被 railway/bus 修饰为可观察到的地点。", "points": ["选词填空", "名词"]}, {"id": "2016-12-3-c-32", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 32, "stem": "", "options": ["A) accessing", "B) conveniences", "C) destined", "D) expanding", "E) industrialized", "F) perceived", "G) practice", "H) process", "I) progress", "J) respective", "K) survived", "L) terminals", "M) undergone", "N) universal", "O) vanished"], "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E) industrialized\n形容词\n【词性槽】with + 空 + America，空作前置定语修饰 America\n【依据】industrialized America = 工业化的美国，呼应首段\"machine civilization\"\n首段铺垫all have __(27)__ changes as they have come into contact with an __(28)__\nmachine civilization.，第二段进一步说阿米什人have lived with __(32)__ America for\nover two and a half centuries，工业化美国正是他们抵抗同质化的对象，与上\n文\"machine civilization\"语义一致。\n【竞争词】C) destined 搭配不成立\ndestined 多用作 be destined to/for，作前置定语修饰国家不自然，且无法承接\"被抵抗的对\n象\"的语义。\nJ) respective 需复数对象\nrespective 表\"各自的\"，必须修饰复数名词指代多方各自所有，单独修饰 America 不合常理。\nN) universal 义不切题\nuniversal America（普遍的美国）不符合常识表达，也未承接前文对工业化、机器文明的\n强调。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2016-12-3-c-33", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 33, "stem": "", "options": ["A) accessing", "B) conveniences", "C) destined", "D) expanding", "E) industrialized", "F) perceived", "G) practice", "H) process", "I) progress", "J) respective", "K) survived", "L) terminals", "M) undergone", "N) universal", "O) vanished"], "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F) perceived\n动词\n【词性槽】are + 空 + by，被动语态过去分词\n【依据】be perceived by sb. to be = 被某人认为是，标准句式\nThe Amish are often __(33)__ by other Americans to be relics of the past即\"阿米什人\n常被其他美国人认为是过去的遗物\"，perceive 作\"认为/视…为\"时正可接 to be 补语。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2016-12-3-c-34", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 34, "stem": "", "options": ["A) accessing", "B) conveniences", "C) destined", "D) expanding", "E) industrialized", "F) perceived", "G) practice", "H) process", "I) progress", "J) respective", "K) survived", "L) terminals", "M) undergone", "N) universal", "O) vanished"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B) conveniences\n名词\n【词性槽】modern + 空 + and，形容词后并列结构中的名词\n【依据】modern conveniences = 现代便利设施，固定搭配\nThey are seen as abandoning both modern __(34)__ and the American dream of\nsuccess and progress.说阿米什人被视作放弃了\"现代便利\"与\"美国梦\"，modern\nconveniences 是英语里常见搭配，正与下文 inconvenient out-dated customs（不便的\n过时习俗）形成 convenience ↔ inconvenient 的呼应。\n【竞争词】I) progress 并列项重复且无法呼应 inconvenient\nprogress 已紧跟在 and the American dream of success and progress 中出现，本空再用\nprogress 会出现\"放弃现代进步与美国成功与进步的梦想\"的语义冗余；且 progress 无法与下文\ninconvenient out-dated customs 形成 convenience/inconvenient 的同根呼应。", "points": ["选词填空", "名词"]}, {"id": "2016-12-3-c-35", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 35, "stem": "", "options": ["A) accessing", "B) conveniences", "C) destined", "D) expanding", "E) industrialized", "F) perceived", "G) practice", "H) process", "I) progress", "J) respective", "K) survived", "L) terminals", "M) undergone", "N) universal", "O) vanished"], "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G) practice\n动词\n【词性槽】who + 空 + the virtues，关系从句中的谓语动词\n【依据】practice the virtues = 践行美德，标准搭配\nthey are good farmers who __(35)__ the virtues of work and thrift.即\"他们是践行勤劳\n与节俭美德的好农夫\"，practice virtues 是常用搭配，与上文阿米什人\"传统而踏实\"的形象\n贴合。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2016-12-3-m-36", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 36, "stem": "According to Chinese officials, their activities in Antarctica lay greater emphasis on scientific research.", "options": null, "answer": "J", "analysis": "【答案】J 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】中国官员+科研优先\n【定位】Chinese officials say the expansion in Antarctica prioritises scientific research, but they also\nacknowledge that concerns about \"resource security\" influence their moves.\n中国官员表示，在南极的扩张以科研为优先，但他们也承认对『资源安全』的考量会影响其\n行动。\n【改写】Chinese officials ↔ Chinese officials\n专名原样复现\nlay greater emphasis on scientific research ↔ prioritises scientific research\n动词同义替换\n【辨邻】 K K 段核心是 We do weather monitoring here and other research 与展示设施，没\n有出现题干所要求的『官员强调科研优先于资源』这种判断句。\nWe do weather monitoring here and other research", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2016-12-3-m-37", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 37, "stem": "Efforts to create one of the world's largest ocean sanctuaries failed because of Russia's obstruction.", "options": null, "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】海洋保护区+俄罗斯阻挠\n【定位】South Korea, which operates state-of-the-art bases here, is increasing its fishing of krill,\nfound in abundance in the Southern Ocean, while Russia recently frustrated efforts to create\none of the world's largest ocean sanctuaries here.\n韩国在此运营着最先进的基地，正在增加磷虾捕捞——南大洋有大量磷虾——与此同时俄罗\n斯近来阻挠了在此设立世界最大海洋保护区之一的努力。\n【改写】Efforts to create one of the world's largest ocean sanctuaries failed ↔\nRussia recently frustrated efforts to create one of the world's largest ocean sanctuaries\n被动表述与主动表述等值\nbecause of Russia's obstruction ↔ Russia recently frustrated\n原因状语与施动者对应", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2016-12-3-m-38", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 38, "stem": "With several monitoring stations operating in Antarctica, Russia is trying hard to counter America's dominance in the field of worldwide navigational facilities.", "options": null, "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】监测站+对抗美国导航\n【定位】At least three Russian stations are already operating in Antarctica, part of its effort to\nchallenge the dominance of the American GPS, and new stations are planned for sites like the\nRussian base, in the shadow of the Orthodox Church of the Holy Trinity.\n已经至少有三座俄罗斯站点在南极运行，这是其挑战美国 GPS 主导地位努力的一部分，并\n计划在如俄罗斯基地等地点建立新站点。\n【改写】With several monitoring stations operating in Antarctica ↔\nAt least three Russian stations are already operating in Antarctica\n数量词上下位对应\ncounter America's dominance in the field of worldwide navigational facilities ↔\nchallenge the dominance of the American GPS\n同义动词+上位概念\n【辨邻】 F F 段核心是 a freshwater reserve the size of Lake Ontario，与题干要求的『多监测\n站对抗美国导航』完全不同主题。\nRussian researchers boast of their recent discovery of a freshwater reserve the size of Lake Ontario", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2016-12-3-m-39", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 39, "stem": "According to geologists' estimates, Antarctica has enormous reserves of oil and natural gas.", "options": null, "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】油气巨量储备+地质学家估计\n【定位】And while assessments vary widely, geologists estimate that Antarctica holds at least 36\nbillion barrels of oil and natural gas.\n尽管各方估算差异较大，但地质学家估计南极至少蕴藏 360 亿桶油气。\n【改写】According to geologists' estimates ↔ geologists estimate that\n名词化与动词形式互换\nenormous reserves of oil and natural gas ↔ at least 36 billion barrels of oil and natural gas\n形容词与具体数字对应\n【辨邻】 D D 段核心是 the biggest reserves of fresh water on the planet，物质对象不同；题\n干明确要求 oil and natural gas，必须用对象词把 D 段排掉。\nthe biggest reserves of fresh water on the planet", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2016-12-3-m-40", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 40, "stem": "It is estimated that Antarctica boasts of the richest reserves of fresh water on earth.", "options": null, "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】淡水最大储量+地球之最\n【定位】Some scientists are examining the potential for harvesting icebergs from Antarctica, which is\nestimated to have the biggest reserves of fresh water on the planet.\n一些科学家正在研究从南极搬运冰山的可能性——南极据估计拥有地球上最大的淡水储量。\n【改写】richest reserves of fresh water ↔ the biggest reserves of fresh water\n最高级形容词替换\non earth ↔ on the planet\n范围介词短语同义\n【辨邻】 F F 段 a freshwater reserve the size of Lake Ontario 只是单个储水点的规模描述，与\n题干 richest reserves of fresh water on earth 的全球最高级判断不同。\na freshwater reserve the size of Lake Ontario", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2016-12-3-m-41", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 41, "stem": "The demand for energy resources may compel renegotiation of Antarctica's treaties before their expiration.", "options": null, "answer": "I", "analysis": "【答案】I 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】能源需求+迫使条约重谈\n【定位】And even before then, scholars warn, the demand for resources in an energy-hungry world\ncould raise pressure to renegotiate Antarctica's treaties, possibly allowing more commercial\nendeavours here well before the prohibitions against them expire.\n学者警告说，甚至在此之前，能源饥渴的世界对资源的需求就可能给重新谈判南极条约施加\n压力，可能允许更多商业活动在禁令到期前就进入南极。\n【改写】The demand for energy resources ↔ the demand for resources in an energy-hungry world\n近乎逐字复现\nmay compel renegotiation of Antarctica's treaties ↔\ncould raise pressure to renegotiate Antarctica's treaties\n情态动词与动作等值\nbefore their expiration ↔ well before the prohibitions against them expire\n名词与从句对应\n【辨邻】 G G 段 The treaty banning mining here ... comes up for review in 2048 只是描述到\n期时间点，并未说能源需求会迫使提前重谈。\nThe treaty banning mining here, shielding coveted reserves of iron ore, coal and chromium, comes\nup for review in 2048", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2016-12-3-m-42", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 42, "stem": "Many countries are racing against each other to increase their business and strategic influence on Antarctica.", "options": null, "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B 段（弱锚点）\n【锚点】多国竞争+商业与战略影响\n【定位】But an array of countries are rushing to assert greater influence here, with an eye not just\ntowards the day those protective treaties expire, but also for the strategic and commercial\nopportunities that already exist.\n但许多国家正争相在此宣示更大的影响力，他们既盯着这些保护条约到期的那天，也盯着已\n经存在的战略与商业机会。\n【改写】Many countries are racing against each other ↔\nan array of countries are rushing to assert greater influence\n主语+动词同义\nbusiness and strategic influence on Antarctica ↔\nthe strategic and commercial opportunities that already exist\n并列名词同义\n【辨邻】 A A 段 Russia has built Antarctica's first Orthodox church、the Great Wall\nStation、India's futuristic new Bharathi base 都是具体例子，而题干 racing\nagainst each other to increase their business and strategic influence 是一个总结\n性判断，必须落到给出『商业+战略』并列判断的 B 段。\nChinese labourers have updated the Great Wall Station\nQ Q 段 Antarctica is geopolitically contested 是更宏观的政治判断，The old days of\nthe Antarctic being dominated by the interests and wishes of white men from\nEuropean, Australasian and North American states are over 强调主导力量的更\n替，没有明确『business + strategic』并列的影响争夺这一具体说法。\nThe reality is that Antarctica is geopolitically contested", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2016-12-3-m-43", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 43, "stem": "Antarctica's harsh natural conditions constitute huge obstacles to the exploitation of its resources.", "options": null, "answer": "H", "analysis": "【答案】H 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】自然条件严酷+开采障碍\n【定位】Beyond the Antarctic treaties, huge obstacles persist to tapping these resources, like drifting\nicebergs that could jeopardise offshore platforms. Then there is Antarctica's remoteness, with\nsome mineral deposits found in windswept locations on a continent that is larger than Europe\nand where winter temperatures hover around minus 55 degrees Celsius.\n除了南极条约，开发这些资源还面临巨大障碍，比如可能危及海上平台的漂浮冰山。再加上\n南极的偏远，一些矿藏位于风大的地方，整个大陆比欧洲还大，冬季气温徘徊在零下 55 度\n左右。\n【改写】Antarctica's harsh natural conditions ↔\ndrifting icebergs that could jeopardise offshore platforms\n上位概念覆盖具体要素\nconstitute huge obstacles to the exploitation of its resources ↔\nhuge obstacles persist to tapping these resources\n动词与动名词同义\n【辨邻】 G G 段 Antarctica's mineral, oil and gas wealth is a longer-term prize 强调资源前\n景，没有正面讲『自然条件造成开采障碍』，与题干 harsh natural conditions\nconstitute huge obstacles 角度不同。\nAntarctica's mineral, oil and gas wealth is a longer-term prize", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2016-12-3-m-44", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 44, "stem": "With competition from many countries, Antarctica is no longer dominated by the traditional white nations.", "options": null, "answer": "Q", "analysis": "【答案】Q 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】白人国家不再主导\n【定位】\"The old days of the Antarctic being dominated by the interests and wishes of white men\nfrom European, Australasian and North American states are over,\" said Klaus Dodds, a\npolitics scholar at the University of London who specialises in Antarctica.\n『南极被来自欧洲、澳新及北美国家的白人之利益与意愿主导的旧日子已经结束，』伦敦大\n学专门研究南极政治的学者克劳斯·多兹说。\n【改写】Antarctica is no longer dominated by the traditional white nations ↔\nThe old days of the Antarctic being dominated by the interests and wishes of white men from\nEuropean, Australasian and North American states are over\n时代终结句与否定状态等值\nWith competition from many countries ↔ Antarctica is geopolitically contested\n竞争场景同义\n【辨邻】 B B 段 an array of countries are rushing to assert greater influence 只是说多国争\n抢，未触及 traditional white nations 不再主导这一历史结论。\nan array of countries are rushing to assert greater influence here", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2016-12-3-m-45", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 45, "stem": "American scientists complain about lack of sufficient money and equipment for their expansion in Antarctica.", "options": null, "answer": "L", "analysis": "【答案】L 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】美国科学家+经费与设备不足\n【定位】But US researchers quietly complain about budget restraints and having far fewer icebreakers\nthan Russia, limiting the reach of the United States in Antarctica.\n但美国研究人员私下抱怨预算受限、破冰船远少于俄罗斯，这限制了美国在南极的活动\n范围。\n【改写】American scientists ↔ US researchers\n国别+身份同义\nlack of sufficient money ↔ budget restraints\n钱不足的两种说法\nand equipment for their expansion in Antarctica ↔\nhaving far fewer icebreakers than Russia, limiting the reach of the United States in Antarctica\n装备上位+扩张范围对应", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2016-12-3-r-46", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 46, "stem": "What do chain smokers think of cigarette packaging?", "options": ["A) Fancy packaging can help to engage new smokers.", "B) It has little to do with the quality or taste of cigarettes.", "C) Plain packaging discourages non-smokers from taking up smoking.", "D) It has little impact on their decision whether or not to quit smoking."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】What do chain smokers think of cigarette packaging? 直接问某人群对某事物的具体看\n法，不含 infer/suggest/imply，是典型细节题。\n【拆句】What do chain smokers think of cigarette packaging? 是标准 what 引导的特殊疑问\n句，主语 chain smokers、谓语 think of、宾语 cigarette packaging，结构平铺、无\n陷阱。\n【定位】Any veteran nicotine addict will testify that fancy packaging plays no role in the decision to\nkeep smoking.\n任何资深烟民都会作证，花哨的包装在是否继续吸烟的决定中起不到任何作用。\n【选项】A) 偷换概念：偷换观点持有者\n原文谈的是\"老烟民对包装的看法\"，A 把视角偷换成\"包装能否拉新烟民\"（engage new\nsmokers），这是 P2 政府视角，不是题干所问 chain smokers 的视角。\nB) 无中生有：原文话题外的属性\nB 项中的 the quality or taste of cigarettes 在全文中没有任何陈述——原文从头到尾\n讨论的是包装与吸烟行为的关系，从未提及香烟本身的品质或口味。\nC) 偷换概念：偷换观点持有者 + 偷换话题对象\nC 项谈的是 Plain packaging discourages non-smokers from taking up smoking，\n对应 discourage new generations from starting in the first place，这是政府视角对\nplain packaging 的功能阐述，而题干问的是 chain smokers 对一般 cigarette\npackaging 的看法。\nD) ✓ 正确\nfancy packaging plays no role in the decision to keep smoking ↔\nIt has little impact on their decision whether or not to quit smoking\nD 项在两端做了等值改写：no role → little impact、keep smoking → whether or\nnot to quit smoking，与预判完全吻合。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2016-12-3-r-47", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 47, "stem": "What has the UK government agreed to do concerning tobacco packaging?", "options": ["A) Pass a law to standardise cigarette packaging.", "B) Rid cigarette cartons of all advertisements.", "C) Subsidise companies to adopt plain packaging.", "D) Reclassify cigarettes according to packaging."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】What has the UK government agreed to do concerning tobacco packaging? 直接问\n政府同意做的一件具体事，是细节复述题。\n【拆句】What has the UK government agreed to do concerning tobacco packaging? 主干为\nWhat has the UK government agreed to do，介词短语 concerning tobacco\npackaging 限定话题范围，结构清晰、无陷阱。\n【定位】But that isn't why the government—under pressure from cancer charities, health workers and\nthe Labour party—has agreed to legislate for standardised packaging.\n但这并不是政府在癌症慈善机构、卫生工作者和工党的压力下同意为标准化包装立法的\n原因。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nhas agreed to legislate for standardised packaging ↔\nPass a law to standardise cigarette packaging\nA 项在动作词（legislate→pass a law）与对象词（standardised\npackaging→standardise cigarette packaging）两端都做了等值改写，与预判一一\n对应。\nB) 偷换概念：用历史动作冒充本次承诺\n原文政府承诺的是 legislate for standardised packaging（立法实现标准化包装），并\n非 Rid cigarette cartons of all advertisements（去掉烟盒上所有广告）；广告禁令在\nbanning advertising 中提及，但那是政府的历史动作，不是 P2 这次新承诺的内容。\nC) 无中生有：杜撰未提及的执行手段\nC 项 Subsidise companies to adopt plain packaging 中\"补贴企业去采用 plain\npackaging\" 在原文中完全没有依据——政府是用立法强制，不是用补贴诱导。\nD) 偷换概念：颠倒手段与目的的指向\n原文 Plain packaging would be another step in the reclassification of cigarettes\nfrom inviting consumer products to narcotics 提到的 reclassification 是\"把香烟从\n消费品重新分类为麻醉剂\"，分类的依据是产品属性而非包装本身；D 项 Reclassify\ncigarettes according to packaging 颠倒了因果，把 plain packaging 推动重新分类的\n过程，偷换成\"按包装来分类香烟\"。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2016-12-3-r-48", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 48, "stem": "What has happened in Australia where plain packaging is implemented?", "options": ["A) Premature death rates resulting from smoking have declined.", "B) The number of smokers has dropped more sharply than in the UK.", "C) The sales of tobacco substitutes have increased considerably.", "D) Cigarette sales have been falling far more quickly than in the UK."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】What has happened in Australia where plain packaging is implemented? 问澳大利亚\n在执行该政策后发生了什么，是事实复述。\n【拆句】What has happened in Australia where plain packaging is implemented? 主句问\nwhat has happened，where 引导定语从句修饰 Australia、说明背景；要识别\"在已执行\n该政策的澳大利亚发生了什么\"这一限制条件。\n【定位】This approach has led over time to a fall in smoking with numbers having roughly halved\nsince the 1970s. Evidence from Australia suggests plain packaging pushes society further\nalong that road.\n这种做法长期以来使吸烟人数下降，自 1970 年代以来吸烟人数大约减半。来自澳大利亚的\n证据表明，plain packaging 会让社会沿这条路走得更远。\n【选项】A) 偷换概念：偷换结果指标\n原文 plain packaging pushes society further along that road 中 that road 指\"吸烟\n人数下降\"，并非\"过早死亡率下降\"——虽然 tobacco is one of the biggest causes of\npremature death in the UK 提到了 premature death，但那是\"为何值得尝试 plain\npackaging\"的论据，不是\"澳大利亚发生的结果\"。\nB) ✓ 正确\nplain packaging pushes society further along that road ↔\nThe number of smokers has dropped more sharply than in the UK\nB 项把 further（更远）转写为 more sharply（更陡），并显化了 that road 所指的英\n国吸烟下降，与预判完全吻合。\nC) 无中生有：杜撰未提及的相关概念\nC 项中的 tobacco substitutes（烟草替代品）在全文中没有任何对应——文中讨论的是\nplain packaging 对吸烟率的影响，而非任何替代产品的销量。\nD) 偷换概念：相近但不等值的指标替换\nD 项 Cigarette sales have been falling far more quickly than in the UK 有两处偏\n差：其一，原文谈的是吸烟者人数（numbers 指吸烟人数），不是 cigarette sales（香\n烟销量）；其二，原文 plain packaging pushes society further along that road 在澳\n大利亚的语境是\"已经实现的结果\"，但 \"销量下降\" 与 \"吸烟人数下降\" 并非同一指标\n——前者衡量市场，后者衡量行为。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2016-12-3-r-49", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 49, "stem": "Why has it taken so long for the UK government to consider plain packaging?", "options": ["A) Prime Minister Cameron has been reluctant to take action.", "B) There is strong opposition from veteran nicotine addicts.", "C) Many Members of Parliament are addicted to smoking.", "D) Pressure from tobacco manufacturers remains strong."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】Why has it taken so long for the UK government to consider plain packaging? 问\"为\n何拖这么久\"，原文有显性陈述，不需推断。\n【拆句】Why has it taken so long for the UK government to consider plain packaging? 主句\nWhy has it taken so long，it 为形式主语，真正主语是不定式 for the UK government\nto consider plain packaging，要识别这一形式主语结构。\n【定位】But strength of will is the missing ingredient where Mr. Cameron and public health are\nconcerned. His attitude to state intervention has looked confused ever since his bizarre\n2006 lament(叹惜) that chocolate oranges placed seductively at supermarket checkouts\nfueled obesity.\n但在 Cameron 先生与公共健康问题上，意志的强度正是缺失的那一味要素。从 2006 年他\n离奇地为超市收银台旁诱人摆放的巧克力橘子\"助长肥胖\"而叹惜以来，他对国家干预的态度\n一直显得迷茫。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nBut strength of will is the missing ingredient where Mr. Cameron and public health are\nconcerned.\n↔ Prime Minister Cameron has been reluctant to take action\nThe government has moved reluctantly into a sensible public health policy ↔\nPrime Minister Cameron has been reluctant to take action\nA 项 reluctant to take action 与 P5 的 strength of will is the missing ingredient 等\n值，与 P6 的 moved reluctantly 直接呼应，是预判的同义改写。\nB) 偷换概念：偷换原文人物的角色\n原文 Any veteran nicotine addict will testify that fancy packaging plays no role in\nthe decision to keep smoking. 中 veteran nicotine addicts 是被引证的旁观者，他\n们\"作证\"包装与戒烟无关，并未\"强烈反对\"立法；B 项把这一旁观引证偷换成了\"老烟民\n构成强烈反对\"。\nC) 无中生有：杜撰人物的私人属性\nC 项 Many Members of Parliament are addicted to smoking 在全文找不到依据\n——原文 MPs voted overwhelmingly in favour of Labour amendments 谈的是\nMPs 投票支持立法，恰恰与\"自己沉迷吸烟\"相反。\nD) 偷换概念：背景常态冒充直接原因\n原文 Naturally, the tobacco industry is violently opposed. 确实说烟草业激烈反对，\n但这是行业的常态立场，作者并未把它列为\"拖延了 5 年\"的直接原因；P5 给出的拖延归\n因是 Cameron 缺意志，并非烟草厂商的压力。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2016-12-3-r-50", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 50, "stem": "What did Cameron say about chocolate oranges at supermarket checkouts?", "options": ["A) They fueled a lot of controversy.", "B) They attracted a lot of smokers.", "C) They made more British people obese.", "D) They had certain ingredients missing."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】What did Cameron say about chocolate oranges at supermarket checkouts? 问\nCameron 对一件具体物品的具体表态，是细节题。\n【拆句】What did Cameron say about chocolate oranges at supermarket checkouts? 主干\nWhat did Cameron say about chocolate oranges，介词短语 at supermarket\ncheckouts 修饰 chocolate oranges、说明地点，结构清晰、无陷阱。\n【定位】His attitude to state intervention has looked confused ever since his bizarre 2006 lament(叹\n惜) that chocolate oranges placed seductively at supermarket checkouts fueled obesity.\n自从他在 2006 年离奇地叹惜超市收银台旁诱人摆放的巧克力橘子\"助长了肥胖\"以来，他对\n国家干预的态度一直显得迷茫。\n【选项】A) 偷换概念：把作者评价说成事件结果\n原文 his bizarre 2006 lament(叹惜) 中的 bizarre 是作者对 Cameron 该言论的评价\n（离奇），而非该言论本身\"引发了大量争议\"——A 项 They fueled a lot of\ncontroversy 把作者主观评价偷换成\"该物品引起了争议\"这一客观事件结果。\nB) 偷换概念：用全文主话题压局部话题\n原文谈的是 chocolate oranges 与 obesity（肥胖）的关系，B 项 They attracted a lot\nof smokers 把对象偷换成 smokers——这一替换利用了全文主话题是吸烟，强行把局\n部话题（肥胖）拉回主话题。\nC) ✓ 正确\nchocolate oranges placed seductively at supermarket checkouts fueled obesity ↔\nThey made more British people obese\nC 项 made more British people obese 与原文 fueled obesity 等值，名词→形容词转\n换 + 动词同义替换，是细节题正确项最常见的形态。\nD) 偷换概念：把抽象比喻误读为字面\n原文 strength of will is the missing ingredient where Mr. Cameron and public\nhealth are concerned 中 missing ingredient 是\"缺失的要素\"的比喻，对应的是\nCameron 缺意志，而非 chocolate oranges 这一具体物品有什么成分缺失。D 项 They\nhad certain ingredients missing 把比喻拽回了字面意义。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2016-12-3-r-51", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 51, "stem": "What is the author's opinion of going to university?", "options": ["A) It is worthwhile after all.", "B) It is simply a waste of time.", "C) It is hard to say whether it is good or bad.", "D) It is too expensive for most young people."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （主旨题）\n【判型】What is the author's opinion of going to university 没有任何细节性钩子，直接问作者\n对整件事的态度/结论，属于全文性的主旨/态度判断。\n【拆句】题干主干 What is the author's opinion of going to university 极简：疑问代词 What 作\n表语 + be 动词 + 主语 the author's opinion + of 引导对象。无从句、无否定、无比较，\n结构平铺直叙，无陷阱。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nYet it still pays to go to university ↔ It is worthwhile after all\nthey have little choice but to embrace university ↔ It is worthwhile after all\nit still pays = worthwhile，still/after all 共同承载\"虽有抱怨但终究如此\"的让步语气，\n正是先抑后扬全文的最大公约数。\nB) 以偏概全\nsimply a waste of time 只对应第 1、2 段的负面铺垫，忽略了 Yet it still pays 之后的\n整片正向论证；且 simply（仅仅是）口吻过强，与作者两面权衡的立场相反。\nC) 与原文相反\n作者立场清晰——\"上大学仍然值得\"——并非\"不好说\"。hard to say whether it is\ngood or bad 把先抑后扬误读成\"作者也没立场\"，掩盖了 Yet it still pays 这一明确\n表态。\nD) 以偏概全\ntoo expensive for most young people 借用首段 £44,000 of debt 的事实，但作者从\n未下结论\"对大多数年轻人太贵\"——相反，他在第 3 段提示 you only start repaying\nwhen you are earning £21,000 a year，承认还款门槛对低收入毕业生友好。把数字事\n实直接抬到\"作者总观点\"是局部冒充整体。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "主旨题"]}, {"id": "2016-12-3-r-52", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 52, "stem": "What does the author say about the employment situation of British university graduates?", "options": ["A) Few of them are satisfied with the jobs they are offered.", "B) It usually takes a long time for them to find a decent job.", "C) Graduates from elite universities usually can get decent jobs.", "D) Most of them take jobs which don't require a college degree."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】What does the author say about the employment situation 问的是作者就具体话题陈\n述了什么事实，无 infer/imply/main idea 标记，属于细节题。\n【拆句】题干主干 What does the author say about the employment situation of British\nuniversity graduates：助动词 does + 主语 the author + 实义动词 say + 介词短语\nabout ... 引出话题。of 短语充当 employment situation 的后置定语，框定主体范围。结\n构清晰、无从句、无陷阱。\n【定位】And graduating doesn't even provide any guarantee of a decent job: six in ten graduates today\nare in non-graduate jobs.\n而且毕业也根本不能保证一份体面的工作：如今十个毕业生里有六个干的是不需要大学学历\n的活儿。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\n原文只提及\"工作是否需要学历\"这一客观属性，从未谈及毕业生对工作\"满意/不满意\"的\n主观感受。\nB) 无中生有\n原文谈的是\"找到的工作够不够体面\"，从未提\"找一份工作要花多久\"，时间维度是凭空引\n入的。\nC) 张冠李戴\n原文确有 Recruiters circle elite universities like vultures 一句，但那是\"招聘者围猎名\n校生\"，是 Q54 的素材，与本题问\"整体毕业生就业现状\"错位。\nD) ✓ 正确\nsix in ten graduates today are in non-graduate jobs ↔\nMost of them take jobs which don't require a college degree\n六成被同义量化为 Most，non-graduate jobs 被关系从句解释，两端语义等值，与预\n判完全吻合。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2016-12-3-r-53", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 53, "stem": "What does the author say is important for university students besides classroom instruction?", "options": ["A) Making sure to obtain an upper-second class degree.", "B) Practical skills they will need in their future careers.", "C) Interactions among themselves outside the classroom.", "D) Developing independent and creative thinking abilities."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】What does the author say is important ... besides classroom instruction 问的是某具体\n方面（非课堂的重要因素）作者说了什么，答案应能从一两句原文中直接读出。\n【拆句】题干 What does the author say is important for university students besides\nclassroom instruction 中 What 同时充当 say 的宾语和后置 is 的主语，是一个省略关系代\n词的嵌套结构——本质等于 \"What [the author says] is important ... besides ...\"。\nbesides 引导的介词短语作状语，表示\"除……之外\"。这是六级常见的嵌入式疑问句，结构\n稍密，但理解为\"作者说什么东西很重要\"即可。\n【定位】It's not just the lectures and tutorials that are important. Education is the sum of what students\nteach each other in between lectures and seminars.\n重要的并不只是课堂讲授与辅导。教育，等于学生们在课堂与讨论课之间相互教给对方的一\n切之和。\n【选项】A) 张冠李戴\nlack a 2.1, i.e., an upper-second class degree 出现在第 4 段说\"用人单位会卡 2.1 学\n位线\"，是顶级岗位招聘的硬门槛、不是\"课堂之外什么对学生重要\"。\nB) 无中生有\n原文从未提及 practical skills / future careers 这一组合；这是套用了\"大学应教实用技\n能\"的常识，与文本无关。\nC) ✓ 正确\nwhat students teach each other in between lectures and seminars ↔\nInteractions among themselves outside the classroom\n\"互相教对方\"是一种最典型的 interaction；\"课堂之间\"自然落在\"课堂之外\"，与预判完\n全吻合。\nD) 无中生有\nDeveloping independent and creative thinking abilities 是国内素质教育语境的高频\n套话，原文从头到尾未出现 independent 或 creative 的对应表述。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2016-12-3-r-54", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 54, "stem": "What is said to be an advantage of going to university?", "options": ["A) Learning how to take risks in an ever-changing world.", "B) Meeting people who will be helpful to you in the future.", "C) Having opportunities of playing a leading role in society.", "D) Gaining up-to-date knowledge in science and technology."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】What is said to be an advantage 用被动 is said 引出，问的是某一项被陈述的具体好处，\n属可在原文直接找到的细节。\n【拆句】题干 What is said to be an advantage of going to university：被动语态 is said to be，\nWhat 作表语；of going to university 是 advantage 的后置定语。语态被动但内容明确，\n无陷阱。\n【定位】Students at university also meet those likely to be in leading jobs in the future, forming\ncontacts for life.\n在大学里，学生还能结识那些未来可能身居要职的人，由此结下一辈子的人脉。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\nLearning how to take risks in an ever-changing world 整句话原文未提；take risks\n与 ever-changing world 都是凭空添加的现代叙事词。\nB) ✓ 正确\nmeet those likely to be in leading jobs in the future, forming contacts for life ↔\nMeeting people who will be helpful to you in the future\n未来身居要职者结成终身人脉 → 对自己将来有帮助，是定位句直接的同义改写。\nC) 过度推断\n原文是 meet those likely to be in leading jobs——上大学的人是\"结识\"将来身居要职\n者，并非自己\"扮演 leading role\"。把 meet leading-job people 推到 play a leading\nrole 多走了一步。\nD) 无中生有\n原文谈\"人脉\"与\"招聘\"，从未提及 up-to-date knowledge in science and\ntechnology；这是\"上大学就学最新知识\"的常识脑补。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2016-12-3-r-55", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 55, "stem": "What can we infer from the last paragraph?", "options": ["A) It is natural for students to make complaints about university education.", "B) Few students are willing to bear the burden of debt incurred at university.", "C) University education is becoming attractive to students who can afford it.", "D) The prestige of the university influences employers' recruitment decisions."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （推断题）\n【判型】What can we infer from the last paragraph 的 infer 是推断题硬标志，需要从末段陈述出\n发做一步推理。\n【拆句】题干 What can we infer from the last paragraph：情态动词 can + 主语 we + 动词 infer\n+ 介词短语 from the last paragraph 指明推理来源。结构平铺直叙，无陷阱。\n【定位】Perhaps the reason why so many universities offer their students so little is they know\nstudying at a top university remains a brilliant investment even if you don't learn anything.\nStudying at university will only become less attractive if employers shift their focus\naway from where someone went to university—and there is no sign of that happening\nanytime soon.\n也许那么多大学之所以给学生提供得那么少，是因为他们清楚：哪怕你什么都没学到，在顶\n级大学念书仍然是一笔绝佳投资。只有当用人单位不再以\"你出自哪所大学\"作为录用焦点\n时，上大学才会变得不再吸引人——而短期内看不到任何这种迹象。\n【选项】A) 答非所问\n原文 School-leavers may moan 只是承接前文情绪化描述，并非\"末段推断\"的核\n心；\"学生抱怨大学是常态\"是文字面表层意思，不算 infer。\nB) 与原文相反\n原文末句明说 they have little choice but to embrace university and the student\ndebt that comes with it——学生别无选择只能接受债务；选项 Few students are\nwilling to bear the burden of debt 把\"被迫接受\"偷换成\"几乎没人愿意承担\"。\"被迫接\n受\" ≠ \"不愿承担\"，且原文是\"接受\"，选项是\"不接受\"，方向相反。\nC) 与原文相反\n原文是\"大学投资回报仍丰、雇主标准未变 ⇒ 上大学仍然吃香\"——是\"持续吸引人\"，\n选项 becoming attractive to students who can afford it 暗示\"正在变得吸引（之前\n不太吸引）\"且把范围缩到\"付得起的人\"，两处都与原文不符。\"付得起的人\"在原文也无\n对应。\nD) ✓ 正确\nStudying at university will only become less attractive if employers shift their focus away from\nwhere someone went to university\n↔ The prestige of the university influences employers' recruitment decisions\nstudying at a top university remains a brilliant investment even if you don't learn anything ↔\nThe prestige of the university influences employers' recruitment decisions\n把\"only ... if ...\"和\"no sign of that\"合起来读，等于雇主当前仍以出身院校论英雄，与预\n判完全吻合。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "推断题"]}], "listeningSharedWith": "2016-12-1"});
